tel 1.0

base profanity
import soundness

info   raw-mode terminal sessions: keypresses and terminal reports as typed events, line editing and menus
detail
    _Profanity_ is the input side of an interactive terminal program. A terminal reports
    everything — a keypress, a resize, a change of focus, a paste, its background colour — as
    escape sequences on standard input, and only in raw mode, which must be turned off again
    however the program exits. Profanity scopes the raw-mode session to a block, decodes the
    input into typed `Terminal.Event`s, and builds line editing and menus over the event
    stream.

    The session is `interactive`. Within it a `Terminal` is a contextual capability: its
    `Terminal#eventIterator` yields the events, its `Terminal#stdio` writes to the screen with
    the terminal's real width and colour depth, and capture checking confines it to the block,
    since raw mode and standard input are torn down when the block ends.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    interactive: terminal ?=>
      terminal.eventIterator().each:
        case Keypress.CharKey(char)               => Out.println(t"pressed $char")
        case Keypress.Ctrl('C')                   => ()
        case Terminal.Info.WindowSize(rows, cols) => Out.println(t"now $cols×$rows")
        case _                                    => ()
    ```

    A `Terminal.Event` is the union of four vocabularies: a clavichord `Keypress` (a character,
    a named editing key, a function key, or any of these held with `Shift`, `Alt`, `Ctrl` or
    `Meta`), a `Terminal.Info` report from the terminal, an `Interrupt` (a POSIX signal such as
    `WINCH`) and a `WindowsSignal`. `Keyboard.Standard` does the decoding, covering legacy and
    VT key sequences, modifier parameters, the kitty keyboard protocol's CSI-u form, bracketed
    paste, focus reports, cursor-position and size reports, and the background-colour reply.

    Optional terminal modes — bracketed paste, focus reporting, mouse tracking, the alternate
    screen, the kitty keyboard protocol — are `Terminal.Feature`s in the `terminalFeatures`
    package. `interactive` turns on every one in scope for the session and off again at its end,
    so a program chooses its modes by importing their givens **by name**: `import pkg.*` does
    not import givens. The `keyboards` package likewise holds the decoders a program may choose
    between.

    On the event stream sit two widgets, each a pure state machine (an event in, a new state
    out) driven by an `Interaction` which renders it: `LineEditor` for a line, or several, of
    text, and `SelectMenu` for a choice among options. Both draw through a `Board`, so the same
    widget renders inline at the cursor (`InlineBoard`), across the whole terminal
    (`TerminalBoard`) or inside a panel of a larger layout.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    interactive: terminal ?=>
      LineEditor().ask: name =>
        SelectMenu(List(t"tea", t"coffee"), t"tea").ask: drink =>
          Out.println(t"$name drinks $drink")
    ```

    Signals reach a session through the `Console` it runs on: a resize or a resumption is
    turned into an event, and the size the launcher measured, where there is one, is recorded
    directly. A `Signal` carries the `Interrupt` or `WindowsSignal` with whatever its sender
    attached.

    `interactive` needs a `Console` — which exoskeleton's `cli` block provides, whether the
    program runs as itself or under an ethereal daemon — and a `Monitor`, a `Probate`, an
    `Environment` and a `Tactic[Terminal.Error]`. The library is one source set,
    `profanity.core`, for the JVM: the session sets raw mode by running `stty`. Everything is
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `keyboards` and `terminalFeatures` choice
    packages merge with the umbrella's own.

refer profanity.interactive
refer profanity.Terminal
refer profanity.LineEditor
refer profanity.SelectMenu
refer profanity.terminalFeatures
refer profanity.Keyboard
refer clavichord.Keypress

entry .interactive   runs a block as a raw-mode terminal session, with a `Terminal` in context
  detail
      `interactive(block)` makes a `Terminal`, puts the terminal in raw mode, applies every
      `Terminal.Feature` in scope, and evaluates the block with the terminal as a contextual
      value; when the block ends, however it ends, it turns the features off in reverse order,
      closes standard input, stops the event stream and waits for the input pump to finish.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import terminalFeatures.{bracketedPasteFeature, focusReportingFeature}

      interactive: terminal ?=>
        terminal.eventIterator().each:
          case Terminal.Info.Paste(text) => Out.println(t"pasted ${text.length} characters")
          case Terminal.Info.LoseFocus   => Out.println(t"focus lost")
          case _                         => ()
      ```

      Raw mode is set with `stty intr undef -echo icanon raw opost` when the `Console`'s
      standard streams are the process's own; under a daemon, whose streams are forwarded from
      a launcher, the launcher has already set the client's terminal and nothing is run. Raw
      mode turns off the terminal driver's handling of `Ctrl`+`C`, so it arrives as the
      keypress `Keypress.Ctrl('C')` rather than as a signal. As soon as raw mode is on, the
      session asks the terminal for its size (`Terminal.reportSize`), since shells do not
      export `LINES` and `COLUMNS` by default; until the reply arrives the terminal is assumed
      to be 80×24.

      It needs a `Console`, a `Monitor` and a `Probate` (the input pump is a daemon task), an
      `Environment` (for `TERMINAL_BG`, `LINES` and `COLUMNS`), an `Every[Terminal.Feature]`
      (empty when no feature is imported) and a `Tactic[Terminal.Error]`, raised when `stty`
      fails because standard input is not a terminal.

      The `Terminal` is an exclusive capability, and capture checking confines it to the block:
      it cannot be returned, captured by a returned closure or stashed in an outer variable,
      since the session it represents has ended when the block does.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def attempt(using Console, Monitor, Probate, Environment, Every[Terminal.Feature])
      :   Terminal =
        interactive(summon[Terminal])   // does not compile
      ```
  refer profanity.Terminal
  refer profanity.terminalFeatures
  refer profanity.Console

entry .terminalStdio   the standard streams a `Terminal` in scope supplies, as a `Stdio`
    A top-level given which makes `Terminal#stdio` the `Stdio` wherever a `Terminal` is in
    context, so `Out.println` inside an `interactive` block writes through the session's
    streams, with a termcap reporting ANSI support, true colour and the terminal's live width.
    It is one canonical instance rather than a choice, so it is a named given at the package's
    top level. It is exported by the umbrella, and since `import soundness.*` does not import
    givens, it is imported by name where it is wanted: `import soundness.terminalStdio`.

entry .Terminal   a live raw-mode terminal session: its event stream, its size, and its output streams
  detail
      A `Terminal` is the capability `interactive` lends its block. It holds the session's
      state: the stream of `Terminal.Event`s an input-pump daemon decodes from standard input,
      the terminal's size as last reported, whether its background is light or dark, and the
      `Stdio` through which to write to it. It is an `Interactivity[Terminal.Event]`, so the
      widgets `LineEditor` and `SelectMenu` take their events from it directly.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      interactive: terminal ?=>
        Out.println(t"${terminal.knownColumns}×${terminal.knownRows}")

        terminal.eventIterator().each:
          case Terminal.Info.WindowSize(rows, columns) => Out.println(t"$columns×$rows")
          case Keypress.Escape                         => ()
          case _                                       => ()
      ```

      The size comes first from the `Console`'s termcap — under a daemon, the launcher measured
      the client's terminal — then from `LINES` and `COLUMNS`, and is updated by every size
      report and every resize signal; `#knownColumns` and `#knownRows` fall back to 80×24 while
      it is unknown. `#mode` is read from the `TERMINAL_BG` environment variable and updated by
      the reply which `terminalFeatures.backgroundColorFeature` asks for.

      A resize is handled in full: on `WINCH` (or `CONT`), the session queries the cursor's
      position and, if the signal did not carry the new size, the size too. The event stream
      receives the `Interrupt` at once, a `Terminal.Info.WindowSize` (at once if the signal
      carried the size, and otherwise when the terminal replies), and a
      `Terminal.Info.CursorPosition` telling an inline renderer where a reflow moved its block.

      A `Terminal` is made only by `interactive`, which tears it down; it is an exclusive
      capability, so it cannot outlive that block.
  refer profanity.interactive
  refer profanity.Terminal.Event
  refer profanity.Keyboard
  refer profanity.Interactivity
  entry .reportSize   the escape sequence which asks the terminal for its size
      Saves the cursor, moves it as far right and down as the terminal allows, asks for its
      position, and restores it: `\e7\e[4095C\e[4095B\e[6n\e8`. The terminal's reply, a
      cursor-position report from the bottom-right corner, decodes as a
      `Terminal.Info.WindowSize`. `interactive` sends it when a session starts, and
      `terminalFeatures.terminalSizeFeature` is it as a feature.
  entry .anchorQuery   the escape sequence which asks where the cursor is: `\e[6n`
      A resize sends it before the size probe, so that an inline renderer learns where the
      terminal's reflow moved the cell its cursor was on. Its reply has the same form as the
      size probe's; the session tells the two apart by the order in which it sent them, and
      reports this one as a `Terminal.Info.CursorPosition`.
  entry .Error   raised when standard input is not attached to a terminal
    detail
        Error 493: _STDIN is not attached to a TTY_. Raised by `interactive` when `stty` fails
        to put the terminal in raw mode, as it does when the program's input is a pipe or a
        file.
  entry .Feature   a terminal mode turned on and off by a pair of escape sequences
    detail
        A `Feature` is an `#enable` sequence and a `#disable` sequence: bracketed paste, focus
        reporting, mouse tracking, the alternate screen, the kitty keyboard protocol. The
        standard ones are givens in the `terminalFeatures` package, and `interactive` summons
        every one in scope as an `Every[Terminal.Feature]`, turning each on for the session
        and off at its end, nested so that the last turned on is the first turned off. A
        one-shot query, such as for the background colour or the size, has an empty
        `#disable`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        given synchronizedOutput: Terminal.Feature =
          Terminal.Feature(t"\e[?2026h", t"\e[?2026l")
        ```

        A feature can also be applied around a block within a session, with `#apply`.
    refer profanity.terminalFeatures
    entry #enable   the escape sequence which turns the mode on
    entry #disable   the escape sequence which turns the mode off; empty for a one-shot query
    entry #apply   runs a block with the mode on, turning it off afterwards however the block ends
        Needs a `Terminal` in context, through whose `Stdio` the sequences are written, so a
        feature can be applied only inside an `interactive` session.

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        interactive: terminal ?=>
          terminalFeatures.alternateScreenFeature:
            Out.println(t"on the alternate screen")
        ```
  entry .Info   a report from the terminal: its size, the cursor, its background, focus, or a paste
    detail
        The events a terminal sends which are not keypresses, decoded by `Keyboard.Standard`:

        ```tabular
        column  Case
        column  Report
        column  Sent
        row  `WindowSize(rows, columns)`  the terminal's size; the `Terminal` records it before passing it on  in reply to `Terminal.reportSize`: at session start, after a resize, or for `terminalFeatures.terminalSizeFeature`
        row  `CursorPosition(row, column)`  where the cursor is, in one-based screen coordinates  in reply to `Terminal.anchorQuery`, or as a `?`-prefixed DECXCPR report
        row  `BgColor(red, green, blue)`  the background colour, with sixteen-bit components; the `Terminal` updates its `#mode` from it  in reply to the query of `terminalFeatures.backgroundColorFeature`
        row  `LoseFocus`, `GainFocus`  a change of focus  with `terminalFeatures.focusReportingFeature`
        row  `Paste(text)`  the whole of a bracketed paste, rather than one keypress per character  with `terminalFeatures.bracketedPasteFeature`
        row  `Redraw`  a request to repaint  never by the terminal: an application puts it on the event stream to wake its event loop after a background task changes what is shown
        ```

        ```scala fuel
        typecheck
        ##
        interactive: terminal ?=>
          terminal.eventIterator().each:
            case Terminal.Info.WindowSize(rows, columns) => Out.println(t"$columns×$rows")
            case Terminal.Info.Paste(text)               => Out.println(text)
            case Terminal.Info.GainFocus                 => Out.println(t"welcome back")
            case _                                       => ()
        ```
  entry .Event   every event a terminal session yields: a keypress, a report, or a signal
      The union `Keypress | Terminal.Info | Interrupt | WindowsSignal`. `Keypress` is
      clavichord's, so that a browser driver can name a keypress without depending on a
      terminal library; a union rather than a sealed trait, since a type in another module
      cannot extend a sealed one here. A match over it is still checked for exhaustivity.
  entry #in   the session's standard input stream
  entry #out   the session's standard output stream
  entry #err   the session's standard error stream
  entry #keyboard   the `Keyboard.Standard` decoder which turns the input into events
      Its `Keyboard.Lookahead` consults the live input: a lone escape character followed at
      once by more input begins a sequence, and one followed by a quiet line for ten
      milliseconds is the `Escape` key.
  entry #mode   whether the terminal's background is light or dark, if known
      An `Optional[Brightness]`, read when the session starts from the `TERMINAL_BG`
      environment variable (as `rrrr/gggg/bbbb` hexadecimal components), and updated when the
      terminal replies to the query `terminalFeatures.backgroundColorFeature` sends. It can
      also be assigned.
  entry #rows   the terminal's height in rows, if known
      Updated by every size report and resize signal, and assignable within the session:
      `terminal.rows = 10`.
  entry #columns   the terminal's width in columns, if known
      Updated by every size report and resize signal, and assignable within the session:
      `terminal.columns = 40`.
  entry #knownColumns   the terminal's width, or 80 while it is unknown
      The conservative fallback is deliberate: drawing for a narrower terminal than the real
      one is benign, while assuming a wider one scatters absolutely-addressed output.
  entry #knownRows   the terminal's height, or 24 while it is unknown
  entry #cap   the session's termcap: ANSI, true colour, and the terminal's live width
  entry #stdio   the session's standard streams, carrying `#cap` as their termcap
      The `Stdio` through which to write to the terminal, with the `Console`'s streams and a
      termcap whose width follows the terminal's. `terminalStdio` makes it the `Stdio` in
      scope wherever the `Terminal` is.
  entry #events   the relay onto which the input pump and the signal handler put events
      A `Relay[Terminal.Event]`. An application may put its own events onto it — such as
      `Terminal.Info.Redraw`, to wake an event loop — and `interactive` stops it when the
      session ends, which ends every iterator over it.
  entry #eventIterator   an iterator over the session's events, in the order they arrived
      Each call drains the shared event queue afresh, so two iterators share the events
      between them rather than each seeing all of them. The iterator is exhausted when the
      session's input ends.
  entry #pumpInput   the daemon task which reads standard input and decodes it into events
      Started with the session, it reads characters from the `Console`'s input, decodes them
      with `#keyboard`, records any size or background-colour report, and puts each event on
      `#events`. However it ends — the session closing standard input, end of input, or a
      decoding failure — it stops the event stream, so a loop over the events sees the end
      rather than blocking for ever. `interactive` waits for it on teardown.

entry .Console   the console a terminal session runs on: its standard streams, and a signal trap
  detail
      `interactive` needs a `Console`: the standard streams the session reads and writes, and
      a way to be told of signals. Exoskeleton's `Cli`, which the `cli` block provides, is
      one, whether the program runs directly or as an ethereal daemon's client, whose streams
      and signals the launcher forwards.

      `Console(stdio)` makes one over any `Stdio`, whose `#trap` ignores every handler, for a
      session which needs no signals.
  refer profanity.interactive
  refer profanity.Signal
  entry .apply   a console over the given standard streams, which receives no signals
  entry #stdio   the console's standard streams
  entry #trap   registers a handler for the signals the console receives
      A `PartialFunction[Signal, SignalResponse]`. A `Terminal` registers one for `WINCH`
      and `CONT`, to track resizes, and turns every other signal into an event. The console
      made by `Console(stdio)` discards the handler.

entry .Keyboard   a decoder from the characters a terminal sends to the keypresses they mean
  detail
      A `Keyboard` turns a lazy `Chain[Char]` of input into a `Chain` of its `#Keypress`
      type. `Keyboard.Standard` is the decoder a `Terminal` uses, and the `keyboards` package
      holds it with two trivial ones as givens to import by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val keys = Keyboard.Standard().process(Chain('h', 'i', '\u001b', '[', 'A', '\r'))
      // CharKey('h'), CharKey('i'), Up, Enter
      ```

      The helpers in the companion are the pieces `Keyboard.Standard` is built from, and are
      public for decoders of related encodings.
  refer profanity.keyboards
  refer clavichord.Keypress
  entry .modified   wraps a key in the modifiers a legacy modifier digit names
      `Keyboard.modified(digit, key)` reads the digit of a sequence such as `\e[1;5A` as one
      plus a bitmask, and wraps the key as `applyModifiers` does: `'5'` is `Ctrl`, so
      `\e[1;5A` is `Ctrl(Up)`.
  entry .applyModifiers   wraps a key in the modifiers a bitmask names: shift 1, alt 2, ctrl 4, meta 8
      The modifiers nest outwards in that order, so a bitmask of 5 makes `Ctrl(Shift(key))`.
      The bitmask is the kitty protocol's modifier value less one.
  entry .csiu   decodes the parameters of a kitty-protocol CSI-u sequence into a keypress
      Takes the characters between `\e[` and the final `u`, of the form
      `codepoint[:shifted[:base]][;modifiers[:event]][;text]`. The codepoints 13, 9, 27 and 8
      or 127 are `Enter`, `Tab`, `Escape` and `Backspace`, which may carry any modifier; any
      other codepoint is a character, made a `Ctrl` keypress with the control modifier and
      upper-cased with the shift modifier.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Keyboard.csiu(List('1', '3', ';', '2'))   // Shift(Enter)
      ```
  entry .ctrlChar   the `Ctrl` keypress for a character, or the plain character if it has no control form
      Letters (of either case) and `@`, `[`, `\`, `]`, `^` and `_` have control forms.
  entry .navigation   the editing key the final letter of a cursor sequence names: `A` is `Up`, `H` is `Home`
      ```tabular
      column  Letter
      column  Key
      row  `A`  `Up`
      row  `B`  `Down`
      row  `C`  `Right`
      row  `D`  `Left`
      row  `F`  `End`
      row  `H`  `Home`
      row  any other  `Escape`
      ```
  entry .vt   the editing key the digit of a VT sequence `\e[n~` names: `2` is `Insert`, `5` is `PageUp`
      ```tabular
      column  Digit
      column  Key
      row  `1`, `7`  `Home`
      row  `2`  `Insert`
      row  `3`  `Delete`
      row  `4`, `8`  `End`
      row  `5`  `PageUp`
      row  `6`  `PageDown`
      row  any other  `Escape`
      ```
  entry .Lookahead   decides whether an escape character begins a sequence or is the `Escape` key
    detail
        The escape key and the start of an escape sequence are the same character; they are
        told apart by whether more input follows closely. `#sequenceFollows` is given the
        rest of the input after an escape and answers whether it belongs to a sequence.

        A terminal writes a whole sequence at once, so on a live terminal already-buffered
        input decides at once, and only a quiet line waits, briefly, for the rest of a
        sequence split across packets (as an SSH connection may split one) before the escape
        is reported as the key. `.tty` is that policy; `.immediate`, in the companion and so
        in implicit scope, is for input which is already complete.
    entry .immediate   for complete input, such as a test or a replay: a sequence follows if anything does
        The `Keyboard.Lookahead` which `Keyboard.Standard()` uses when no other is given. It
        tests whether the rest of the input is empty, which on a live terminal would block
        until the next keypress; use `.tty` there.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Keyboard.Standard().process(Chain('\u001b')).head   // Escape
        ```
    entry .tty   for a live terminal: buffered input decides at once, a quiet line waits up to a deadline
        `Keyboard.Lookahead.tty(stdio, deadline)` asks the `Stdio` whether input is ready,
        polling each millisecond for up to `deadline` milliseconds (ten by default). The
        `Terminal`'s own keyboard uses it.
    entry #sequenceFollows   whether the input after an escape character continues an escape sequence
  entry .Standard   the decoder for what terminals send: keys, modifiers, CSI-u, pastes and reports
    detail
        `Keyboard.Standard()` decodes into `clavichord.Keypress | Terminal.Info`, with a
        `Keyboard.Lookahead` from context to tell the `Escape` key from the start of a
        sequence (`Keyboard.Lookahead.immediate` unless another is in scope). It reads:

        - control characters: backspace (`\b` or DEL), tab, line feed or carriage return as
          `Enter`, and other C0 codes as `Ctrl` of their letter;
        - `\eO` followed by a letter as a `FunctionKey`;
        - cursor and VT editing sequences (`\e[A`, `\e[3~`), with legacy modifier digits
          (`\e[1;5A`, `\e[3;2~`);
        - kitty keyboard protocol sequences, `\e[…u`, through `Keyboard.csiu`;
        - a bracketed paste, `\e[200~…\e[201~`, as one `Terminal.Info.Paste`;
        - cursor-position reports, as `Terminal.Info.WindowSize` (the plain form, which a
          `Terminal` reclassifies when it is answering `Terminal.anchorQuery`) or
          `Terminal.Info.CursorPosition` (the `?`-prefixed form);
        - focus reports, as `Terminal.Info.LoseFocus` and `Terminal.Info.GainFocus`;
        - the reply to an OSC 11 background-colour query, as `Terminal.Info.BgColor`;
        - any other CSI sequence as an `EscapeSeq`, and every other character as a `CharKey`.

        A malformed report is dropped, and decoding continues after it.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        Keyboard.Standard().process(Chain('\u001b', '[', '1', '3', ';', '2', 'u')).head
        // Shift(Enter)

        Keyboard.Standard().process(Chain('\u001b', '[', '?', '1', '2', ';', '3', '4', 'R')).head
        // Terminal.Info.CursorPosition(12, 34)
        ```
    entry #Keypress   `clavichord.Keypress | Terminal.Info`
    entry #process   decodes a lazy stream of input characters into keypresses and reports
        Lazy in both directions: each event is decoded as the characters it needs arrive, so a
        live terminal's input can be processed as it is typed.
  entry #Keypress   the type of event the keyboard decodes into
  entry #process   decodes a lazy stream of input characters into a lazy stream of events

entry .keyboards   the keyboard decoders, each a given to import by name
  detail
      A `Keyboard` given chooses how input characters are decoded. `import soundness.*` brings
      none of these in, since `import pkg.*` does not import givens; one is imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import keyboards.rawKeyboard
      ```

      A `Terminal` always decodes with its own `Keyboard.Standard`; these givens are for
      code which processes input itself.
  refer profanity.Keyboard
  entry .rawKeyboard   passes every character through undecoded, as a `Char`
  entry .numericKeyboard   passes every character through undecoded, as its `Int` code
  entry .standardKeyboard   the full decoder, `Keyboard.Standard`, as a given
      Needs a `Monitor` and a `Probate` in scope, and uses the implicit-scope
      `Keyboard.Lookahead.immediate`, which suits complete input; a live terminal needs
      `Keyboard.Lookahead.tty`, as the `Terminal`'s own keyboard has.

entry .terminalFeatures   the standard terminal modes, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Each given is a `Terminal.Feature`, which `interactive` turns on for the session and off
      at its end. A session has only the modes whose givens are in scope, and since `import
      pkg.*` does not import givens, each is imported by name:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import terminalFeatures.{kittyKeyboardFeature, mouseTrackingFeature}

      interactive: terminal ?=>
        terminal.eventIterator().each:
          case Keypress.Shift(Keypress.Enter) => Out.println(t"shift-enter")
          case _                              => ()
      ```

      The effect is best seen by adding modes one at a time:

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label No features: keys and the size report only
        code
            interactive: terminal ?=>
              terminal.eventIterator().each(event => Out.println(event.toString))
      frame
        label Bracketed paste: a paste is one event
        code
            import terminalFeatures.bracketedPasteFeature

            interactive: terminal ?=>
              terminal.eventIterator().each(event => Out.println(event.toString))
              // Terminal.Info.Paste(…) in place of one CharKey per character
      frame
        label And the kitty protocol: modified keys are distinguishable
        code
            import terminalFeatures.{bracketedPasteFeature, kittyKeyboardFeature}

            interactive: terminal ?=>
              terminal.eventIterator().each(event => Out.println(event.toString))
              // Shift(Enter) arrives distinct from Enter
      frame
        label And the alternate screen: the scrollback is left untouched
        code
            import terminalFeatures.{bracketedPasteFeature, kittyKeyboardFeature}
            import terminalFeatures.alternateScreenFeature

            interactive: terminal ?=>
              terminal.eventIterator().each(event => Out.println(event.toString))
      ##
      ```
  refer profanity.Terminal.Feature
  refer profanity.interactive
  entry .bracketedPasteFeature   bracketed paste: a paste arrives as one `Terminal.Info.Paste`
      `\e[?2004h` on, `\e[?2004l` off. Without it, pasted text is indistinguishable from
      typing, and a pasted newline is an `Enter`.
  entry .focusReportingFeature   focus reporting: `Terminal.Info.GainFocus` and `LoseFocus` events
      `\e[?1004h` on, `\e[?1004l` off.
  entry .mouseTrackingFeature   mouse tracking, in SGR form: button presses as escape sequences
      `\e[?1000h\e[?1006h` on, `\e[?1006l\e[?1000l` off. The reports are not decoded into
      mouse events: they arrive as `Keypress.EscapeSeq`s.
  entry .alternateScreenFeature   the alternate screen buffer, which leaves the scrollback untouched
      `\e[?1049h` on, `\e[?1049l` off: a full-screen interface draws on a separate screen, and
      the terminal's previous contents return when the session ends.
  entry .kittyKeyboardFeature   the kitty keyboard protocol: unambiguous keys and modifiers as CSI-u
      `\e[>1u` pushes the protocol's disambiguation flag, and `\e[<u` pops it. With it,
      `Shift`+`Enter`, `Ctrl`+`I` and `Tab`, or `Escape` and `Alt` combinations are reported
      distinctly, decoded by `Keyboard.csiu`. A terminal which does not implement the protocol
      ignores both sequences.
  entry .backgroundColorFeature   a one-shot query for the background colour, answered as `Terminal.Info.BgColor`
      Sends `\e]11;?\e\` when the session starts and nothing at its end. The reply updates
      `Terminal#mode`, so a program can choose light or dark colours.
  entry .terminalSizeFeature   a one-shot query for the size, answered as `Terminal.Info.WindowSize`
      Sends `Terminal.reportSize` when the session starts and nothing at its end.
      `interactive` already sends it once on a real terminal, so this matters mainly for a
      session over forwarded streams.

entry .Board   the surface a widget draws on: a cursor to move, text to put, and a size
  detail
      Every renderer draws through a `Board`, positioning only with `#move` and never by
      printing escape codes, so the same widget code draws inline at the cursor
      (`InlineBoard`), across the whole terminal (`TerminalBoard`) or into a clipped region of
      a larger layout. Coordinates are local to the surface, zero-based `Ordinal`s, the column
      before the row.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def banner(board: Board^): Unit =
        board.move(Prim, Prim)
        board.clear()
        board.put(t"Ready")
        board.flush()
      ```

      A board is a stateful, exclusive capability: drawing changes it, so every drawing
      operation is an `update` method, callable only through an exclusive reference
      (`Board^`).

      With a `Terminal` in context, `.inlineBoard`, in the companion, supplies an
      `InlineBoard` over it; a panel supplies its own board in a nearer scope, which takes
      precedence, which is how a `LineEditor` asked for inside a panel draws within it.
  refer profanity.InlineBoard
  refer profanity.TerminalBoard
  refer profanity.Interaction
  entry .inlineBoard   with a `Terminal` in context, an `InlineBoard` at its cursor
      In implicit scope, so a widget asked for in an `interactive` block draws in place at the
      prompt with nothing imported.
  entry #width   the surface's width in cells
  entry #height   the surface's height in cells
  entry #move   moves the cursor to a cell, by column and then row
  entry #put   writes text, or styled `Teletype` text, at the cursor, advancing it
  entry #clear   erases the whole surface
  entry #clearLine   erases the current row from the cursor onwards
  entry #cursor   shows or hides the hardware cursor
  entry #showCaret   leaves the visible cursor at a cell, once a frame is drawn
      A buffered surface may move the cursor as it writes a frame out, so a widget which shows
      a caret places it after `#flush`.
  entry #flush   commits a frame; nothing on a surface which is not buffered

entry .InlineBoard   a `Board` at the terminal's cursor, drawn with relative motion so it stays at the prompt
  detail
      An `InlineBoard` draws a widget in place, where the cursor was when it began: row 0 is
      that line and column 0 its left edge, and each `#move` becomes relative vertical motion
      and an absolute column, so the widget redraws over itself rather than at the top of the
      screen, and the scrollback above it is left alone. It keeps track of its own cursor,
      counting a newline or a wrap at `#width` as a move to the next row. `put` of a
      `Teletype` writes its plain text.

      With a `Terminal` in context, `Board.inlineBoard` supplies one over it, so this is the
      board `LineEditor` and `SelectMenu` use in an `interactive` block.
  refer profanity.Board
  entry .apply   an inline board over a terminal, at its known width and height
  entry #width   the width at which text wraps
  entry #height   the height the board was made with

entry .TerminalBoard   a `Board` over the whole terminal, with absolute positioning and the live size
  detail
      Every `#move` is an absolute cursor position, so the board is the whole screen, as a
      full-screen interface on the alternate screen wants. It keeps no cursor of its own,
      letting the terminal advance it as text is written, and reads its size when asked, so
      it follows a resize.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import terminalFeatures.alternateScreenFeature

      interactive: terminal ?=>
        val board = TerminalBoard(terminal)
        board.clear()
        board.move(Prim, Prim)
        board.put(t"Full screen")
      ```

      The board captures its terminal only to read its size, so its capture set is
      `{terminal.rd, caps.any}`: the size thunks given to the constructor may only read.
  refer profanity.Board
  entry .apply   a board over a terminal, sized live, or one of a fixed size over the `Stdio` in scope
      `TerminalBoard(terminal)` reads `Terminal#knownColumns` and `Terminal#knownRows` each
      time it is asked its size, and writes through the terminal's `Stdio`.
      `TerminalBoard(width, height)` has a fixed size and writes through the `Stdio` in
      context.

entry .Interaction   how a widget is drawn and answered: renders each state, and decides what submits it
  detail
      An `Interaction[answer, question]` drives a widget — a `question`, such as a `LineEditor`
      or a `SelectMenu` — over a stream of events: it renders each state with `#render`,
      passes each event to the widget to make the next state, and stops when an event submits
      (`#submits`, by default `Enter`) or dismisses (`Escape`, `Ctrl`+`C`, `Ctrl`+`D`, or
      the end of the events). The answer is `#result` of the final state, or `Unset` if the
      widget was dismissed.

      The companion's `.selectMenu` and `.lineEditor` render through the `Board` in scope, so
      `ask` on either widget needs nothing imported. An interaction of one's own can render
      differently, or not at all, which is how a widget's logic is tested without a terminal:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val quiet = new Interaction[Text, SelectMenu[Text]]:
        def render(old: Optional[SelectMenu[Text]], menu: SelectMenu[Text]): Unit = ()
        def result(menu: SelectMenu[Text]): Text = menu.current

      val menu = SelectMenu(List(t"alpha", t"beta", t"gamma"), t"alpha")
      quiet(Iterator(Keypress.Down, Keypress.Enter), menu)(_(_))   // t"beta"
      ```

      `#react` lets an interaction change the state in answer to an event before the widget
      sees it, as a completer does on `Tab`.
  refer profanity.LineEditor
  refer profanity.SelectMenu
  refer profanity.Board
  entry .selectMenu   draws a `SelectMenu` on the `Board` in scope, one option per row, `>` at the current one
      Hides the cursor while the menu is shown, redraws the whole menu on each change —
      counting an option wider than the board as the rows it wraps onto — and clears it and
      restores the cursor when the menu is answered. Needs a `Showable` for the options.
  entry .lineEditor   draws a `LineEditor` on the `Board` in scope, with its caret, wrapping wide text
      Redraws the editor's text each frame and places the caret where
      `LineEditor.cursorPosition` says, counting embedded newlines, wrapping and wide
      characters; submits as the editor's `LineEditor.Mode` decides, and moves to a fresh line
      below the text when it is answered.
  entry #before   called once before the first state is drawn; does nothing by default
  entry #render   draws a state, given the state drawn before it, if any
  entry #after   called once the widget is answered or dismissed; does nothing by default
  entry #result   the answer a final state gives
  entry #submits   whether an event submits the widget in its current state; by default, `Enter`
  entry #react   changes the state in answer to an event before the widget sees it; by default, nothing
      Called for every event which neither submits nor dismisses. An interaction which
      completes a word on `Tab` overrides it:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val completing = new Interaction[Text, LineEditor]:
        def render(old: Optional[LineEditor], editor: LineEditor): Unit = ()
        def result(editor: LineEditor): Text = editor.value

        override def react(editor: LineEditor, event: Terminal.Event): LineEditor =
          event match
            case Keypress.Tab => LineEditor(t"${editor.value}ography")
            case _            => editor
      ```
  entry #recur   the event loop: renders, reads an event, and submits, dismisses or continues
      Not usually called directly: `#apply` calls it after `#before`.
  entry #apply   runs the widget over an iterator of events, from an initial state, to an answer or `Unset`
      `interaction(events, state)(step)`, where `step` makes the next state from a state and
      an event — `_(_)` for a `Question`, whose `apply` is that step. Calls `#before`, runs
      the loop, then `#after`.

entry .Interactivity   a source of events for widgets to read from, such as a `Terminal`
  detail
      `LineEditor#ask` and `SelectMenu#ask` take their events from the
      `Interactivity[Terminal.Event]` in context, which in an `interactive` block is the
      `Terminal` itself. They hand their continuation an `Interactivity` over the same
      iterator they read from, so a widget asked for in the continuation carries on where the
      first stopped, rather than missing events read ahead.
  refer profanity.Terminal
  entry .apply   an `Interactivity` which hands out an iterator already being read
  entry #eventIterator   the iterator of events to read from

entry .Question   a widget as a state machine: an event in, the next state out
  detail
      `LineEditor` and `SelectMenu` are `Question`s. A question's `#apply` takes one
      `Terminal.Event` and gives the next state; an `Interaction` draws the states and
      decides when one is the answer.
  refer profanity.LineEditor
  refer profanity.SelectMenu
  refer profanity.Interaction
  entry .Error   raised when the user dismisses a widget instead of answering it
    detail
        Error 372: _the user dismissed an interaction_. Raised by `LineEditor#ask` and
        `SelectMenu#ask` when the user presses `Escape`, `Ctrl`+`C` or `Ctrl`+`D`, or the
        input ends, before submitting.
  entry #apply   the next state, after an event

entry .LineEditor   an editable line of text, or several, with a cursor: a widget asked for its text
  detail
      A `LineEditor` is the state of a text field: its `#value` and the `#position` of the
      cursor within it. Each keypress makes a new editor, with the usual editing keys:
      characters insert at the cursor; `Backspace` and `Delete` remove either side of it;
      `Left`, `Right`, `Home` and `End` move it; `Ctrl` with `Left` or `Right` moves by
      words; `Ctrl`+`W` deletes the word before the cursor and `Ctrl`+`U` everything before
      it.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      interactive: terminal ?=>
        LineEditor(t"draft").ask: text =>
          Out.println(t"You entered: $text")
      ```

      `#ask` runs the editor in the terminal, drawing it inline at the cursor (or in the
      `Board` in scope), and passes the submitted text to its continuation; dismissing it
      raises `Question.Error`.

      In `LineEditor.Mode.Multiline` the arrow keys and `Home` and `End` move between and
      within lines, and a predicate on the text decides whether `Enter` submits or inserts a
      newline; `Shift`+`Enter`, which needs `terminalFeatures.kittyKeyboardFeature` for the
      terminal to report it, always submits.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val script = LineEditor(mode = LineEditor.Mode.Multiline(_.ends(t";")))
      ```
  refer profanity.Question
  refer profanity.Interaction
  refer profanity.SelectMenu
  entry .Mode   whether `Enter` submits the editor, or inserts a newline
    detail
        `SingleLine` (the default): `Enter` submits. `Multiline(submit)`: `Enter` submits if
        `submit` holds of the text so far, and otherwise inserts a newline; `Shift`+`Enter`
        always submits; `Up` and `Down` move between lines, and `Home` and `End` go to the
        ends of the current line.
  entry .cursorPosition   the row and column of a character offset in text laid out a given width
      `LineEditor.cursorPosition(text, position, columns)` lays the text out as a grid of
      cells does: by grapheme cluster, a wide character such as `中` taking two cells and a
      combining mark none, wrapping a cluster which would overrun the width, and moving to
      the next row at each newline. For plain text without newlines it is `position/columns`
      and `position%columns`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      LineEditor.cursorPosition(t"中b", 2, 10)   // (0, 3)
      LineEditor.cursorPosition(t"ab中", 3, 3)   // (1, 2)
      ```
  entry #value   the text being edited
  entry #position0   the cursor offset given to the constructor, or `Unset` for the end of the text
  entry #mode   whether `Enter` submits or inserts a newline, as a `LineEditor.Mode`
  entry #position   the cursor's offset within the text
  entry #submitsOn   whether an event submits this editor, according to its `LineEditor.Mode`
  entry #apply   the editor after a keypress
      A keypress the editor does not handle, or one which would move the cursor out of
      range, leaves it as it was.
  entry #ask   runs the editor in the terminal and passes the submitted text to a continuation
      Needs an `Interactivity[Terminal.Event]` (the `Terminal`, in an `interactive` block), an
      `Interaction[Text, LineEditor]` (the companion's `Interaction.lineEditor`, drawing on
      the `Board` in scope) and a `Tactic[Question.Error]`, raised if the editor is
      dismissed. The continuation's result is `ask`'s; within it, a further widget reads the
      same events.

entry .SelectMenu   a choice among options, moved through with the arrow keys: a widget asked for its choice
  detail
      A `SelectMenu` is the state of a menu: its `#options` and the `#current` one. `Up` and
      `Down` move by one, stopping at the ends, and `Home` and `End` go to the first and the
      last.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      interactive: terminal ?=>
        SelectMenu(List(t"alpha", t"beta", t"gamma"), t"alpha").ask: choice =>
          Out.println(t"You chose $choice")
      ```

      `#ask` draws the menu inline at the cursor (or in the `Board` in scope), one option per
      row with `>` before the current one, and passes the option chosen with `Enter` to its
      continuation; dismissing it raises `Question.Error`.
  refer profanity.Question
  refer profanity.Interaction
  refer profanity.LineEditor
  entry #options   the options, in the order they are shown
  entry #current   the option the menu is on
  entry #apply   the menu after a keypress
  entry #ask   runs the menu in the terminal and passes the chosen option to a continuation
      Needs an `Interactivity[Terminal.Event]` (the `Terminal`, in an `interactive` block), an
      `Interaction[item, SelectMenu[item]]` (the companion's `Interaction.selectMenu`, which
      needs the options to be `Showable`) and a `Tactic[Question.Error]`, raised if the menu
      is dismissed.

entry .Signal   a signal as delivered to a program, with the terminal size or deadline its sender attached
  detail
      A POSIX signal carries nothing of its own, but a launcher forwarding one can: an
      ethereal client sends `WINCH` and `CONT` with the terminal's size, which the daemon at
      the other end of a socket cannot measure, and a Windows close, logoff or shutdown event
      with the time the system allows before it ends the process regardless. A signal raised
      on the process itself carries nothing.

      `Console#trap` handlers receive `Signal`s:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def handler: PartialFunction[Signal, SignalResponse] =
        case Signal(Interrupt.Winch, columns, rows, _) => SignalResponse.Accept
        case Signal(_: WindowsSignal, _, _, deadline)  => SignalResponse.Accept

      Signal(Interrupt.Winch, 80, 24).size   // (80, 24)
      ```

      A signal shows as its short name: `WINCH`, `CTRL_LOGOFF`.
  refer profanity.Interrupt
  refer profanity.WindowsSignal
  refer profanity.SignalResponse
  entry .showable   shows a signal as its short name, such as `WINCH` or `CTRL_CLOSE`
  entry #interrupt   the signal: an `Interrupt` (a POSIX signal) or a `WindowsSignal`
  entry #columns   the terminal's width, if the sender attached it
  entry #rows   the terminal's height, if the sender attached it
  entry #deadline   the time before the system ends the process, if the sender attached it
  entry #size   the terminal's size as `(columns, rows)`, if the sender attached both

entry .SignalResponse   how a signal handler answers: `Accept`, `Reject` or `Defer`
    `Accept` says the handler has dealt with the signal; `Reject` that it has not, so the
    signal's default action applies (a launcher dispatching to no handler answers `Reject`);
    `Defer` that it will be dealt with later.

entry .Interrupt   a POSIX signal, `HUP` to `SYS`, with its name and number
  detail
      The thirty POSIX signals as an enum: `Hup`, `Int`, `Quit`, `Ill`, `Trap`, `Abrt`, `Bus`,
      `Fpe`, `Kill`, `Usr1`, `Segv`, `Usr2`, `Pipe`, `Alrm`, `Term`, `Chld`, `Cont`, `Stop`,
      `Tstp`, `Ttin`, `Ttou`, `Urg`, `Xcpu`, `Xfsz`, `Vtalrm`, `Prof`, `Winch`, `Io`, `Pwr`
      and `Sys`. It is also a `Terminal.Event`: a `Terminal` puts every signal its console
      receives onto its event stream. `UnixSignal` is another name for it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Interrupt.Winch.name   // t"SIGWINCH"
      Interrupt.Term.id      // 15
      t"winch".as[Interrupt]   // Interrupt.Winch
      ```
  refer profanity.Signal
  refer profanity.WindowsSignal
  entry .decoder   decodes a signal from its short name, in any case: `WINCH` or `winch`
      The name without its `SIG` prefix. A name which is not a signal's throws
      `IllegalArgumentException`, rather than raising an error through a tactic.
  entry .encodable   encodes a signal as its short name, `WINCH`
  entry .showable   shows a signal as its short name, `WINCH`
  entry #shortName   the name without its prefix, in capitals: `WINCH`
  entry #name   the full name: `SIGWINCH`
  entry #id   the signal's number, in Linux's numbering: `HUP` is 1, `TERM` 15, `CHLD` 17
      Numbered from 1 in the enum's order, skipping 16. The numbers are Linux's; macOS and
      the BSDs number signals from `URG` onwards differently.

entry .UnixSignal   another name for `Interrupt`, the POSIX signals
    Both the type alias and its companion, so `UnixSignal.Winch` and `case _: UnixSignal`
    work as they do with `Interrupt`. The enum is named `Interrupt` because `Signal` is the
    delivered signal with its payload.

entry .WindowsSignal   a Windows console control event: `Ctrl`+`C`, `Ctrl`+`Break`, close, logoff, shutdown
  detail
      The five events a Windows console delivers in place of POSIX signals: `CtrlC`,
      `CtrlBreak`, `Close`, `Logoff` and `Shutdown`. A launcher forwards them, and a `Close`,
      `Logoff` or `Shutdown` `Signal` carries the deadline before the system ends the process.
      It is also a `Terminal.Event`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      WindowsSignal.Close.shortName   // t"CTRL_CLOSE"
      ```
  refer profanity.Signal
  refer profanity.Interrupt
  entry .decoder   decodes an event from its short name, such as `CTRL_C` or `CTRL_LOGOFF`
      In any case. A name which is not an event's throws `IllegalArgumentException`, rather
      than raising an error through a tactic.
  entry .encodable   encodes an event as its short name, such as `CTRL_BREAK`
  entry .showable   shows an event as its short name, such as `CTRL_BREAK`
  entry #shortName   the name a launcher uses: `CTRL_C`, `CTRL_BREAK`, `CTRL_CLOSE`, `CTRL_LOGOFF`, `CTRL_SHUTDOWN`

entry .CtrlChar   matches a C0 control character as the letter or symbol `Ctrl` makes it from
  detail
      An extractor: `case CtrlChar(letter)` matches a character whose code, plus 64, is `@`,
      a capital letter, or one of `[`, `\`, `]`, `^` and `_`, and binds that character, with
      a singleton-union type. `Keyboard.Standard` uses it to decode `\u0003` as
      `Keypress.Ctrl('C')`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      '\u0003' match
        case CtrlChar(letter) => letter   // 'C'
        case _                => '?'
      ```
  entry .unapply   the control character's letter or symbol, or `None` for any other character
